A Flutter widget test cannot tap the real iOS notification-permission dialog or verify a real scheduled notification. Widget tests run in Flutter’s test environment; the permission dialog and notification delivery belong to iOS, outside the Flutter widget tree.

Use a deterministic widget test around an injected notification seam:

```dart
testWidgets('grants permission and schedules a notification',
    (tester) async {
  final notifications = FakeNotifications(permissionGranted: true);

  await tester.pumpWidget(
    MaterialApp(home: ReminderPage(notifications: notifications)),
  );

  await tester.tap(find.text('Enable notifications'));
  await tester.pump();

  expect(find.text('Notifications enabled'), findsOneWidget);
  expect(notifications.scheduled, hasLength(1));
  expect(notifications.scheduled.single.title, 'Reminder');
});
```

Then cover the real iOS boundary separately with an integration/device test. Flutter integration tests still cannot tap arbitrary native permission dialogs as Flutter widgets; permission must be granted through simulator/device automation, such as `simctl privacy`, before launching the test. Actual notification appearance should be verified through XCUITest or a manual device workflow, since it is rendered by iOS rather than Flutter.
